<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/inspector-clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/inspector-clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/inspector-clipboard.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-expertise/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Umpire &amp; Neutral Appraiser Services</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Umpire Services in Delaware</h1><p class="lede">When two appraisers can’t agree, the umpire decides the remaining items. We serve as a neutral umpire on Delaware panels where we represent neither side.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/start-your-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Neutral decision-maker</dd></div><div><dt>Selected by</dt><dd>Both appraisers</dd></div><div><dt>Award</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>What an umpire does</h2>
<p>Under a standard appraisal clause, the two appraisers choose an umpire. If they can’t agree on one, either party can usually ask a court of record to appoint one. The umpire’s job is limited: review the items still in dispute and decide them, so that two of the three panel members can sign an award. As an insurance appraisal umpire in Delaware, we do exactly that — no more, no less.</p>
<h2>How we run the umpire process</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Disclosure first.</strong> Before accepting, we disclose any prior relationships with either appraiser, the insured or the carrier.</li>
<li><strong>Defined issues.</strong> We ask the appraisers to agree on what is settled and list what’s left, with each side’s figure.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection.</strong> We inspect the property when the disputed items require it.</li>
<li><strong>Fair hearing.</strong> Each appraiser presents their support. We ask questions, review documents and evaluate the evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Clear award.</strong> Our award states each decided item, so everyone can see how the total was reached.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When we decline</h2>
<p>We won’t serve as umpire if we have represented either side on a related claim, if a conflict exists or if the dispute is really about coverage, which belongs with the courts. Neutrality is the whole value of the role.</p>
<h2>Selecting an umpire</h2>
<p>Appraisers often look for an umpire with construction pricing experience, familiarity with the type of property and no ties to either side. We’re glad to provide our background and fee schedule to both appraisers before anyone commits.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most appraisal clauses split the umpire’s fee equally between the insured and the insurer. Check your policy wording.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can you be an umpire on a claim where you were contacted by one side?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Only if that contact didn’t involve advice or representation on the claim, and only after full disclosure to both appraisers.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the appraisers can’t agree on an umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most policies allow either party to ask a court to appoint one. That adds time and cost, so agreeing on a neutral umpire early helps.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="cards cards-3"><a class="card rv" href="/appraisal-expertise/appraisal-clause-appraiser/"><span class="card-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, (max-width:1100px) 50vw, 380px" width="960" height="640" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="card-ico"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"